[38;5;231m⏺[39m The change itself is one line — [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m, [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m. But session lifetime is security
  posture, so I want your explicit go-ahead before editing rather than after.

  What it means in practice: a session token stays valid 16× longer. An unattended logged-in browser, a stolen session
  cookie, or a revoked user's live session all keep working for up to 8 hours instead of 30 minutes. There's no
  idle-vs-absolute distinction in this config — [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES[39m is the only knob, and [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m just reads it
  — so this extends the whole window, not just the idle grace period.

  If that's the intent (long-lived internal tooling, users complaining about being logged out mid-task), say go and I'll
  make the one-line change. If the actual goal is "stop kicking people out while they're working," an idle timeout that
  refreshes on activity would give you that without an 8-hour absolute window — that's a larger change, so tell me
  which you want.

